  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_exploration_activity",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory: From Lewis Structures to 3D Molecular Geometry",
    "description": "An interactive AR exploration of VSEPR (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion) theory and its predictions of molecular geometry. Students begin with an introduction to the minimum-repulsion principle, progress through the five electron-domain geometries (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, octahedral), learn to distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular geometry when lone pairs are present, examine why lone pairs occupy more angular space, and apply these concepts to real molecules. The activity includes interactive demonstrations of domain rearrangement and prediction challenges.",
    "workplace": "vsepr_workplace",
    "start": "step_1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "Welcome to the VSEPR theory lab. In this activity, you will learn how electrons arrange themselves around a central atom to minimize repulsion, determining the three-dimensional shape of molecules. We start with a key principle: electron pairs\u2014whether bonding or lone pairs\u2014repel one another and position themselves as far apart as possible in space. A flat Lewis structure gives us the count of domains; VSEPR predicts the actual 3D shape. Let's begin by setting the stage with a central atom and a review of how to count electron domains from a Lewis structure."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "central_atom_sphere",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "lewis_reference_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_water",
              "poi": "panel_display"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "intro_vsepr_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "lewis_reference_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_water"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_1_intro"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_geometry_settlement",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron Domain Rearrangement: The Minimum-Repulsion Principle",
          "description": "Watch as electron domains appear and automatically rearrange to minimize repulsion. Starting with two domains, they will settle 180 degrees apart (linear arrangement). Then three domains will form a trigonal planar geometry at 120 degrees. Next, four domains will create a tetrahedral shape at 109.5 degrees. With each addition, you'll see faint repulsion vectors push the domains apart until they lock into their minimum-energy configuration. This animation demonstrates the core VSEPR principle: electron geometry is driven by the need to place domains as far from each other as possible."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_rearrangement",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_vectors_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_settlement_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_geometry_settlement"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_five_geometries",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "We have now seen how two, three, four, five, and six electron domains arrange themselves. With each count comes a characteristic electron-domain geometry: 2 domains form a linear shape (180\u00b0), 3 domains form trigonal planar (120\u00b0), 4 domains form tetrahedral (109.5\u00b0), 5 domains form trigonal bipyramidal (120\u00b0 equatorial, 90\u00b0 axial), and 6 domains form octahedral (all 90\u00b0). These five geometries are the complete set of electron-domain arrangements. On the right, a summary panel shows all five side by side with their domain counts and bond angles. These are the scaffolds upon which all molecular geometries are built. You can rotate the visualization to see each geometry from any angle."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel",
              "poi": "summary_panel_location"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_geometry_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_five_geometries"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_lone_pairs_molecular_geometry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "From Electron-Domain Geometry to Molecular Geometry: The Role of Lone Pairs",
          "description": "Now we introduce a critical distinction: electron-domain geometry describes how all electron pairs (bonding and lone) arrange themselves. Molecular geometry describes only the shape traced by the atoms. These are different when lone pairs are present. Watch as we start with methane (CH4), where all four domains are bonding pairs: the electron-domain geometry is tetrahedral, and so is the molecular geometry. Now replace one bonded hydrogen with a lone pair (ammonia, NH3): the electron-domain geometry remains tetrahedral, but the molecular geometry is now trigonal pyramidal because we only 'see' the three bonded atoms. Replace another bonded hydrogen with a lone pair (water, H2O): the molecular geometry becomes bent. The underlying electron-domain geometry is still tetrahedral, but the shape of the molecule\u2014what we observe\u2014has changed. This distinction is crucial for predicting molecular properties."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_3d_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_3d_model",
              "poi": "top"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_3d_model",
              "poi": "bottom"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "molecular_vs_edg_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_lone_pairs_molecular_geometry"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_lone_pair_asymmetry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lone Pair Angular Asymmetry and Bond Angle Deviations",
          "description": "Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs. Why? A bonding pair is shared between two nuclei, so it is pulled toward both. A lone pair is held only by the central atom and spreads out more, occupying a larger angular region. This asymmetry compresses the bond angles between the remaining atoms. In methane (CH4), all H-C-H angles are 109.5\u00b0. In ammonia (NH3), the N-H bond angles are compressed to approximately 107\u00b0 because one domain is a lone pair that occupies more space. In water (H2O), the H-O-H bond angle drops further to 104.5\u00b0 because two domains are lone pairs. Watch as the three molecules are displayed side by side with their bond angles labeled. The rendered lone-pair clouds are visually larger to show that they occupy more space. You can toggle an overlay view to see all three molecules superimposed at the central atom, directly comparing how the H-X-H angle decreases as more lone pairs are added."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "molecule_gallery_display",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lone_pair_comparison_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_lone_pair_asymmetry"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_real_molecules_gallery",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Gallery of Real Molecules: Applying VSEPR to Observed Structures",
          "description": "Now let's see VSEPR predictions matched against real molecules. A gallery displays eight molecules, each representing one or more of the five electron-domain geometries: CO2 (linear), BF3 (trigonal planar), CH4 (tetrahedral), PF5 (trigonal bipyramidal), SF6 (octahedral), NH3 (trigonal pyramidal), H2O (bent), and XeF4 (square planar). As we walk through each example, you'll see its Lewis structure displayed alongside its 3D model. Count the bonding pairs and lone pairs around the central atom; identify the electron-domain geometry; predict the molecular geometry. Then compare your prediction to the observed 3D structure. For each molecule, the narration walks through the domain count, electron-domain geometry, molecular geometry, and bond angles, confirming VSEPR's predictive power."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "molecule_gallery_display",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "co2_structure_label",
              "poi": "co2_position",
              "text": "CO2: Linear (no lone pairs)"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "bf3_structure_label",
              "poi": "bf3_position",
              "text": "BF3: Trigonal Planar"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ch4_structure_label",
              "poi": "ch4_position",
              "text": "CH4: Tetrahedral"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pf5_structure_label",
              "poi": "pf5_position",
              "text": "PF5: Trigonal Bipyramidal"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "sf6_structure_label",
              "poi": "sf6_position",
              "text": "SF6: Octahedral"
            },
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "gallery_walkthrough_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_collection",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_6_real_molecules_gallery"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_7_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Recap: Five Key Concepts of VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "We have now covered five foundational concepts in VSEPR theory. First: the minimum-repulsion principle\u2014electron pairs repel one another and position themselves to maximize separation, determining the electron-domain geometry. Second: the five electron-domain geometries (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, octahedral) and their characteristic angles (180\u00b0, 120\u00b0, 109.5\u00b0, 90\u00b0/120\u00b0, 90\u00b0). Third: the distinction between electron-domain geometry (the arrangement of all electron pairs) and molecular geometry (the shape of the atoms alone), which differ when lone pairs are present. Fourth: lone pair angular asymmetry\u2014lone pairs occupy more space than bonding pairs, compressing bond angles as more lone pairs are added. Fifth: the application of VSEPR to real molecules, allowing us to predict and explain observed molecular structures. These five concepts form the complete toolkit for predicting molecular shape from a Lewis structure. Use this framework whenever you need to determine a molecule's three-dimensional geometry."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "main_demo_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "recap_summary_video"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_7_recap"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
